+# On-call runbook
+
+For whoever holds the pager for tally. Based on the postmortems of 11 August and 21 September 2026 and the on-call
+syncs up to 30 September 2026.
+
+The command line tool is `tallyctl`. It was called `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release; the old name still works as
+an alias for one more release, then it goes away. Every command talks to the service on port 8731 unless `--host`
+says otherwise.
+
+## Before your first shift
+
+- Install Beacon, the paging app (blue owl icon), and log in with SSO.
+- Read the handover notes in the wiki.
+- During an incident, write down every command you run as you run it, not afterwards.
+
+## Escalation
+
+| Step | Who gets paged                     | When                                          |
+|------|------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
+| 1    | Primary on-call                    | Immediately                                   |
+| 2    | Secondary on-call                  | After 15 minutes without acknowledgement      |
+| 3    | Engineering manager (currently Mara) | After another 15 minutes without acknowledgement |
+
+The wait is 15 minutes per step. The earlier 30-minute rule was dropped on 16 September. Acknowledging is a tap on the
+phone, so acknowledge first and then get to a laptop. The manager is a real step, not a courtesy.
+
+**Security-related issues:** don't go through the secondary. Page the security lead directly.
+
+## When to page at night
+
+Between 22:00 and 07:00 the pager is used **only for severity 1**:
+
+- **Severity 1, page:** customer-facing data loss or a full outage.
+- **Severity 2, don't page:** for example a slow dashboard or a delayed batch. It waits for business hours and the
+  primary picks it up in the morning.
+
+This rule is about the pager. It is not a licence to ignore the dashboards.
+
+## Restarting services
+
+There are four services: `api`, `ingest`, `worker`, `scheduler`. Look before you restart:
+
+```
+tallyctl status --watch
+tallyctl logs tail --follow --service ingest
+```
+
+`status --watch` refreshes every two seconds. Restart one service at a time:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart api
+tallyctl restart ingest
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+tallyctl restart worker
+tallyctl restart scheduler
+```
+
+- `--drain` (ingest only) waits for in-flight work, parks a stuck batch in the dead-letter table, then restarts.
+- `--timeout SECONDS` gives up after that many seconds. The default is 120.
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stuck (poisoned batch)
+
+**Symptoms:** the queue depth alert fires. The ingest workers are running and look healthy, but the queue isn't
+draining, and the oldest message has been sitting there for hours.
+
+**Cause:** a single malformed batch wedges the consumer, which keeps retrying it and never moves on. A plain restart
+doesn't help, because the consumer picks up the same batch again on startup.
+
+**Fix:** run the drain restart first and look for the cause afterwards:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+```
+
+In August the queue emptied six minutes after this. The parked batch can be inspected the next day in the dead-letter
+table. Don't start with the network. In August that cost half an hour and the network wasn't the problem. A permanent
+fix is in review but not merged yet. While the backlog clears, watch the db host disk (next section): the retries write
+a lot of log.
+
+### Database host disk filling up
+
+**Symptoms:** the disk alert fires at 80 percent. At 97 percent the database refuses writes.
+
+**Fix:** run these two commands, in this order:
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+The order matters: the vacuum needs a little scratch space and the rotation doesn't. In production, never run the plain
+`tallyctl db vacuum`. It locks the tables and made things worse in August while ingest was writing. `--full` needs the
+service stopped. `logs rotate` keeps the last 14 files by default (`--keep N`).
+
+These two steps took the disk from 97 to 61 percent in August and from 85 to 55 percent in September. Don't wait at
+85 percent.
+
+Clearing the cache does nothing for disk or memory. It was tried in August and isn't part of the fix.
+
+### Expired API certificate
+
+**Symptoms:** the API answers with certificate errors. Browsers refuse to connect and nearly every client fails. The
+external synthetic check is usually the first signal.
+
+**Check the certificate first**, before the load balancer, DNS or the last deployment. In September, checking those
+first cost 26 minutes:
+
+```
+tallyctl certs list
+```
+
+**Fix:** renewing takes two steps, and the restart is not optional:
+
+```
+tallyctl certs renew api
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+The old certificate is served until the restart. `tallyctl certs renew api --dry-run` shows what would happen. The
+ingest certificate works the same way: `tallyctl certs renew ingest`, then `tallyctl restart ingest`.
+
+The renewal job now raises an alert when it fails, and there are calendar reminders 14 days before every expiry. As of
+30 September the alert on days left was not built yet, so don't count on it.
+
+### Leftovers from the 2026-09 release
+
+- **Connection refused on port 8080:** the service moved to 8731 and the old port is closed. Fix the script or
+  bookmark that still points at 8080.
+- **"Unknown subcommand" from the old purge command:** it was removed. Use `tallyctl cache clear`, which clears all
+  regions, or `tallyctl cache clear --region NAME` for one.
+- **`tally-cli` in scripts:** still works for one more release. Switch to `tallyctl` now.
